  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 349 AM CDT Sat May 16 2026\n\nA very dry air mass will remain firmly in place across the \nNorthland for today, yielding sunny skies and near to above \nnormal temperatures, though nowhere near as warm as we got \nyesterday. Clouds and a weak secondary cold front will keep \ntemperatures along the Borderlands down in the 50s, which is \nbelow normal. West to northwest winds will not be as strong as \nyesterday, but still breezy at 10 to 15 mph, with gusts reaching\nup to 25 to 30 mph. These winds, combined with afternoon \nrelative humidity dropping into the 20 to 25 percent range south\nof U.S. Highway 2, will produce near-critical fire weather \nconditions. While not quite as dangerous as yesterdays \nconditions, it will still be a day to not do any outdoor \nburning. We will be sending out a special weather statement for \ntoday, but a Red Flag Warning is possible if conditions that are\ndrier and windier than forecast develop. \n\nOn Sunday, the pattern shift we have been discussing for a few \ndays begins making changes to our local weather. A low pressure \nsystem developing over eastern Colorado and western Kansas will \nbegin to trek directly toward the area, initiating significant \nchanges. Winds will veer northeast tonight and then increase out\nof the east on Sunday, gusting up to 30 to 35 mph on land and \nup to 35 to 40 knots over western Lake Superior, where gales are\nexpected. This strong onshore flow will keep temperatures \nunseasonably cool for most of northeast Minnesota, where high \ntemperatures should only get into the 50s. Farther south it will\nbe warmer, but we will have increasing clouds and rain moving \nin from the southwest to keep temperatures even there in the \n60s. Rain showers and embedded storms will develop and spread \nfrom southwest to northeast during the afternoon and into the \nevening. Sunday night will bring widespread rain and heavier \nthunderstorms as deep moisture surges into the region. As a \nsurface warm front boundary is likely to stall across central MN\ninto northwest WI, most of the southern third of the CWA is \nlikely to be under elevated instability with deep layer shear of\n40kts or better, which may support severe storms capable of \nlarge hail and damaging winds. Precipitable water values of an \ninch and a quarter or higher are over the 95th percentile of \nclimatology for this time of year. Given how dry we have been \nlately this rainfall will be much needed, but the dry soils may\nnot be able to absorb it as quickly as it falls, and localized \nflooding is also a concern. The surface low tracks across the \nsoutheastern portions of the area on Monday, which will bring \nanother wave of widespread rain and storms Monday and Monday \nnight. The primary risk of severe weather shifts into northwest \nWisconsin during the afternoon and evening. There will be plenty\nof mid level instability for large hail and wind, and if we can\nget surface based instability, tornadoes also become a \npossibility. Widespread storm-total rainfall from Sunday \nthrough Tuesday will reach 1 to 3 inches, with localized amounts\nup to 4 inches possible. Again, this heavy rainfall could cause\nlocalized minor flooding, especially near the Boundary Waters \nwhere waters remain high from previous rainfall. \n\nThis storm system finally pulls off to the east Monday night \nthrough Tuesday, pulling much colder air into the region from \nthe northwest. This colder air will be accompanied by gusty \nnorthwest winds of 15 to 25 mph, with gusts up to 30 mph. \nWraparound rain showers will persist, mainly over northeast \nMinnesota. A few wet snowflakes could mix in with the rain \nshowers, mainly along and north of the Iron Range. Tuesday and \nWednesday will be the coolest days of the week, with highs only \nin the 40s to low 50s. The cool air brought in by this system \nwill persist through Wednesday and Thursday, with some rather \nchilly overnights expected. Frost will be possible for parts of \nthe area both Wednesday and Thursday mornings. \n\nFor the end of the week, the upper-level flow becomes more zonal\nwith several shortwaves in that flow, allowing temperatures to\nslowly warm up into next weekend with high temperatures climbing\nback to near to above normals for mid May. This pattern will\nalso bring us some intermittent chances for precipitation.",
